<%# The keep control itself. Locals: painting, kept:, autofocus:

    Rendered ONLY inside the private frame's response, never in the cached page.
    That is not a style choice — `button_to` emits a CSRF token, and generating
    one starts a session, so rendering this in the public HTML puts a
    `Set-Cookie` on `/` and `/days/:date` and breaks story 0007's whole
    arrangement. `test/integration/favorites_test.rb` catches it in one line.

    What the cached page carries instead is the static placeholder in
    `daily/_day.html.erb`: the same glyph at the same size in the same box, with
    no form around it. So the mark is painted at first paint and the rail never
    shows an empty 44px hole where the habit mechanic should be; the interactive
    control replaces it when the frame lands.

    No visible words, so the `aria-label` is the whole announcement — see
    `decisions/0010`. `aria-pressed` is unchanged; it never depended on the label.

    `autofocus` is true ONLY on the write responses. Turbo replaces the whole
    frame on submit, which destroys the focused button and drops focus to <body>;
    autofocus puts it back, and focus landing on a toggle is also what makes a
    screen reader announce the new state. On the initial lazy GET it is false —
    a fragment arriving unbidden must not steal focus, which is the same reason
    there is no aria-live here. %>
<%= button_to favorite_path(painting), method: (kept ? :delete : :post),
      form: { class: "rail__form" },
      class: "rail__act",
      autofocus: autofocus,
      aria: {
        pressed: kept,
        label: kept ? "Remove #{painting.title} from your collection"
                    : "Keep #{painting.title} in your collection"
      } do %><%= render "favorites/keep_glyph", filled: kept %><% end %>
